<article class="env-lemma" id="0CCK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CCK">Lemma <span data-tag="0CCK">53.2.3</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a nonconstant morphism of curves over $k$. If $Y$ is normal, then $f$ is flat. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Pick $x \in X$ mapping to $y \in Y$. Then $\mathcal{O}_{Y, y}$ is either a field or a discrete valuation ring (Varieties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B8Y" data-tag="0B8Y">33.43.8</a>). Since $f$ is nonconstant it is dominant (as it must map the generic point of $X$ to the generic point of $Y$). This implies that $\mathcal{O}_{Y, y} \to \mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is injective (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0CC1" data-tag="0CC1">29.8.8</a>). Hence $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is torsion free as a $\mathcal{O}_{Y, y}$-module and therefore $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is flat as a $\mathcal{O}_{Y, y}$-module by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0539" data-tag="0539">15.22.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>